  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Annulate Masked Bee (Hylaeus annulatus) is a small, solitary bee belonging to the Plasterer Bee family, native to New England and eastern North America. These bees are named for their habit of lining their nesting tunnels with a cellophane-like secretion, creating a waterproof coating.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Yellow facial markings",
      "description": "Distinctive yellow or pale markings on the face and clypeus (lower face area) form a characteristic 'mask' pattern",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Typically 4-6mm in length, making them one of the smallest bee species in New England",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth, shiny body",
      "description": "Dark body with a glossy appearance and banded abdominal segments that may show pale coloration",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Annulate Masked Bees prefer open woodlands, gardens, and flowering meadows throughout New England where they can access small cavities in dead stems, hollow plant stalks, or bee hotels for nesting.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their tiny size, these bees are efficient pollinators that visit a wide variety of flowers, and they are completely harmless to humans with little to no aggressive behavior.",
